\title{pcmprsk: Parametric Competing Risks Regression}
\description{
The \pkg{pcmprsk} package provides tools for fitting parametric
regression models for cumulative incidence functions in the presence
of competing risks.
}
\details{
The implemented methodology is based on the generalized odds rate
(GOR) transformation model of Jeong and Fine (2007) with flexible
parametric baseline subdistribution functions. Both two-parameter and
three-parameter Gompertz baseline distributions and a three-parameter
modified logistic baseline distribution are supported, allowing monotonic
as well as unimodal (peaked) subdistribution hazard shapes.
The package includes maximum likelihood estimation,
statistical inference, prediction of cumulative incidence functions,
and estimation of long-term cure fractions.

The cumulative incidence function (CIF) is a fundamental measure
for quantifying the probability of a specific event in the presence
of competing risks. Rather than modeling cause-specific or
subdistribution hazards, the implemented regression model directly
parameterizes the cumulative incidence function. The generalized
odds rate transformation provides a flexible framework that includes
the proportional hazards and proportional odds models as special cases.

Baseline subdistribution functions can be specified using either
two-parameter or three-parameter Gompertz distributions, or a
three-parameter modified logistic distribution.
The three-parameter formulation extends the standard Gompertz model by
accommodating non-monotonic, unimodal subdistribution hazards
(Haile et al., 2016), making it suitable for applications in which
event rates increase to a peak before declining.
The three-parameter modified logistic model (Cheng, 2009) is adapted
from the four-parameter logistic function for dose-response curves and
accommodates sigmoidal cumulative incidence functions with an inflection
point, which cannot be captured by the Gompertz model.

Model parameters are estimated by full maximum likelihood, enabling
likelihood-based inference and prediction of cumulative incidence
functions for user-specified covariate values. When an improper
baseline distribution is fitted, the model also supports estimation
of long-term event probabilities and cure fractions.
}
